# Exploring Normality Through Dynamics

A base expansion can be studied as a moving point. Multiplying by $b$ and keeping only the fractional part shifts the digit stream, while equal subintervals encode finite blocks. Begin with the visual orbit below and then reveal the definitions as needed.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

<details><summary>Background: fractional parts and equidistribution</summary>

For $y\in\mathbb R$, $\{y\}=y-\lfloor yfloor$. A sequence $(u_n)$ is interval-equidistributed when the fraction of its first $N$ terms in every $[a,c)$ tends to $c-a$. See [equidistributed sequences](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equidistributed_sequence) for broader context.

</details>

## From orbit cells to blocks

The next $k$ base-$b$ digits are the code $\lfloor b^k\{b^n x\}floor$. Explore the corresponding partition, then inspect an exact implementation.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the core proof</summary>

For every code $A$, the floor identity gives
$$
\lfloor b^k\{b^n x\}floor=A
\iff
\{b^n x\}\in[A/b^k,(A+1)/b^k).
$$
Equidistribution assigns this interval its length $1/b^k$ as limiting visit frequency. Thus every length-$k$ block has frequency $1/b^k$, which is base-$b$ normality.

</details>

