<details><summary>Why finite evidence is not a proof</summary>

Normality quantifies over every block length and an infinite limit. Every finite decimal approximation is rational and eventually has a periodic multiplicative orbit. Small observed discrepancies therefore illustrate only the tested scale. For analytic context, read about [Weyl's criterion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equidistribution_theorem#Weyl's_criterion).

</details>

## The open frontier

The connector theorem identifies a sufficient condition but does not establish it for $\pi$, $e$, or $\sqrt2$. Irrationality, algebraicity, transcendence, and algebraic independence do not themselves control the needed orbit distribution. Future progress would require bounds for the exponential averages $N^{-1}\sum_{n<N}e^{2\pi i m b^n x}$ or another route to equidistribution.
